{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"let-property-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Let a property track","description":"Takes a landlord from an empty home to a tenant moved in through gated steps - legal and safety readiness, pricing and marketing, fair screening, then the agreement, deposit and check-in.","category":"real-estate","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","review","ship"],"role":["individual","operations-manager"],"subject":["real-estate"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","notes"],"output":["plan","checklist","copy","message"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["legal"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["landlord","letting","tenant-screening","check-in-inventory","first-let"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["check-landlord-obligations","set-asking-rent","write-rental-listing","screen-tenant-applications","write-tenant-welcome-pack"],"personas":["property-manager"]},"args":[{"name":"property","description":"The property - type, size, condition, furnished or not, mortgage or lease restrictions you know of, and when it is available.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"landlord","description":"Your situation - first let or experienced, self-managing or using an agent, the kind of tenancy you want, how much time you have, and any rent figure in mind.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"country","description":"Country and state or region. It decides the safety checks, deposit rules, tenancy types and notices that apply.","type":"string","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"readiness","file":"steps/01-readiness.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"price-and-market","file":"steps/02-price-and-market.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"screening","file":"steps/03-screening.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"agreement-and-move-in","file":"steps/04-agreement-and-move-in.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Lets one home from empty to occupied the way a careful self-managing landlord should: legally ready before marketing, priced on evidence, a tenant chosen by written criteria applied to everyone, and a move-in documented well enough to settle any deposit question years later.\n\n<property>\n{{property}}\n</property>\n\n<landlord>\n{{landlord}}\n</landlord>\n\nLocation: {{country}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nEach step produces its artifacts and a gate checklist, then stops for the landlord's approval. The landlord may come back days later with new information; pick up at the step they name. Steps 2 to 4 depend on real events (comparables, enquiries, applicants, signed documents): ask for them and never invent them. Mark every legal or compliance point for {{country}} `[CHECK locally]` rather than stating it as fact, and suggest a landlord association, the local authority or a lawyer where the stakes are high. Never suggest criteria, wording or practices that discriminate against applicants. If the landlord asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining planning steps in one reply and state the choice made at each skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"readiness","file":"steps/01-readiness.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Legal and safety readiness\n\nMake sure the home can lawfully be let before anyone sees an advert.\n\n1. List the permissions to confirm: mortgage lender consent to let, leasehold or association rules, landlord insurance, any landlord registration or property licence. Mark each `[CHECK locally]`.\n2. List the safety and standards checks typically required before a tenancy starts in {{country}}: gas and electrical safety, smoke and carbon monoxide alarms, fire safety for furnished items, energy rating or minimum standard, water and legionella risk, and any habitability standard. Mark each `[CHECK locally]` with who usually does it.\n3. Walk the property against the landlord's notes: repairs to finish, items to remove or replace, keys, manuals, meter locations.\n4. Decide with the landlord: self-manage, let-only agent or full management, with the trade-offs in time, cost and risk.\n5. Write the gate checklist: permissions confirmed, certificates booked or held, repairs done, management choice made, tenancy type chosen.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"price-and-market","file":"steps/02-price-and-market.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Price and market\n\nSet the rent on evidence and launch an honest advert.\n\n1. If no comparable rents are given, ask for at least three recent comparable lets or listings with rent, size, condition and days listed, and stop. Do not guess a rent.\n2. Adjust the comparables openly and recommend a launch rent and a review rule (for example review after 14 days with fewer than a set number of enquiries).\n3. Draft the advert: accurate description, rent, deposit, availability, any lawful restrictions, and no wording that discourages protected groups (avoid \"no benefits\", \"professionals only\", \"no children\" or similar unless lawful and checked locally).\n4. Plan viewings: grouped slots, the same information and questions for everyone, safety for the landlord and the outgoing tenant if occupied.\n5. Write the gate checklist: rent agreed, advert approved, photos taken, viewing plan set, enquiry log ready.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"screening","file":"steps/03-screening.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Screening and selection\n\nChoose a tenant fairly and defensibly.\n\n1. Write the screening criteria before reading applications: affordability measure, acceptable income evidence, references, referencing or credit check with consent, guarantor rule, occupancy limit tied to the property.\n2. Ask for the applicants' information (by label, not full names) and compare each against the criteria as meets, does not meet or unknown. Never infer or use protected characteristics.\n3. List the checks to complete for the chosen applicant, including any right-to-rent or identity check required `[CHECK locally]` and rules on holding deposits and fees.\n4. Draft a short offer message to the chosen applicant and a courteous decline for others that gives no discriminatory reason.\n5. Write the gate checklist: criteria on file, checks completed with consent, decision note written, holding deposit handled lawfully, applicant data kept only as long as needed.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"agreement-and-move-in","file":"steps/04-agreement-and-move-in.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","text":"# Step 4: Agreement, deposit and move-in\n\nStart the tenancy with the paperwork right.\n\n1. List the tenancy agreement points to settle: tenancy type and term, rent and payment date, deposit amount and where it will be held, repairs and reporting, access with notice, pets, subletting, ending the tenancy. Recommend a reputable standard agreement for {{country}} reviewed by a lawyer or landlord association rather than writing one from scratch `[CHECK locally]`.\n2. List the documents and information the tenant must receive at or before the start in {{country}} (for example certificates, deposit protection information, guides or prescribed notices) `[CHECK locally]`, with deadlines to confirm.\n3. Plan the check-in: a detailed, dated inventory with photos room by room, meter readings, keys counted, both parties signing, and a short period for the tenant to add comments.\n4. Draft a welcome message with how to pay rent, report repairs and reach the landlord in an emergency.\n5. Write the final checklist: agreement signed, deposit protected and information given, documents served with proof, inventory signed, rent schedule set up, diary dates for certificates and renewals."}]}
